+## Experimental semantic binding comparison
+
+The generator's opt-in `--experimental-semantic-model` switch captures immutable
+class-parse facts before Java type resolution, plans managed projections, and
+lowers them into the existing C# peer emitter. It is a compiler front-end spike,
+not a replacement emitter or a production compatibility guarantee. The default
+generation path is unchanged.
+
+The expanded frontend can additionally consume
+`--experimental-api-descriptor=FILE`, produced by the isolated
+[`jvm-api-descriptor`](../../tools/jvm-api-descriptor/README.md) Kotlin/ASM tool.
+It validates physical JVM slots and overlays official Kotlin visibility and
+type contracts on the unadjusted input. C# retains CLR ownership, naming,
+projection, and marshaling; this does not replace the entire existing emitter
+or bytecode helper analysis.
+
+Semantic accessibility distinguishes supported public/protected APIs,
+necessary hidden infrastructure, excluded declarations, and unverified facts.
+It considers enclosing visibility, Kotlin source visibility, `RestrictTo`,
+`VisibleForTesting`, and `ApiStatus.Internal`. Required CLR implementation
+slots are retained without advertising them as ordinary supported API.
+Reports preserve the reasons; public bytecode alone is not a public Kotlin API.
+Source-private and package-private classes are excluded rather than promoted
+into public nested peers, including inside restricted owners. Native facts
+remain intact for dependency/slot planning. Necessary restricted CLR contracts
+remain hidden infrastructure rather than ordinary supported APIs.
+
+The semantic policy also recognizes the legacy `zz...` implementation naming
+convention in the reviewed Google namespaces, and Google Play Services internal
+packages. These declarations remain hidden native infrastructure rather than
+being promoted into ordinary public API or blindly deleted. Explicit
+`obfuscated="false"` exceptions survive semantic lowering and later metadata
+application; they do not override Kotlin/source restriction annotations.
+The full-config canonical catalog uses this same classifier and tracks required
+hidden signature closure separately from public contract conflicts.
+
+`--experimental-generic-facades` separately emits typed ordinary managed views
+over one canonical raw peer and requires a reference to the updated
+`Microsoft.Android.Kotlin` runtime. It preserves generic class/method parameters,
+inherited substitutions, fields, nested collections, reference arrays, and
+generic suspend/Flow results. Views do not register JNI peers. Erased bounds
+are checked at runtime, not claimed as complete CLR F-bound enforcement;
+unsupported mutable existential projections remain raw with diagnostics.
+
+Representable native bounds additionally constrain class/method type parameters
+through the shared `IJavaProjection<out TPeer>` interface. Canonical peers expose
+themselves, and ordinary views expose their existing peer; no extra JNI owner is
+registered. A separately named `XMappedView` companion preserves custom
+converter-based representations without dropping runtime native-bound checks.
+Converted, dependent, recursive and unavailable peer bounds remain runtime-only,
+with native bounds in generated type-parameter documentation and reasons in the
+projection report. Host regressions compile both valid and invalid consumers;
+the reference app exercises the contracts, mapped conversions and default
+interface implementations under trimming/R8 and NativeAOT.
+
+The spike handles bridge-backed generic overrides, preserves Kotlin value-class
+projection attributes through XML adjustment, and retains singleton fields that
+otherwise conflict with nested types or expose private JVM types. Native JNI
+descriptors remain separate from projected managed types. An adjacent
+`<adjusted-xml-path>.semantics.json` records projection decisions. Report schema 2
+stores immutable original XML in the adjacent `.native.xml` file named by
+`BinaryFactsFile`, avoiding a giant JSON scalar for whole-corpus graphs.
+The switch requires unadjusted `api-source="class-parse"` input.
+
+Whole-corpus generation grouped by canonical Maven owner is documented in
+[`tests/BindingProjection/Corpus`](../../../../tests/BindingProjection/Corpus/README.md).
+It compiles each coherent graph once and emits real per-group implementations,
+including CLR reference cycles, without shipping the intermediate bootstrap.
+
+The corpus driver now defaults to artifact-level assemblies. The low-level
+emitter accepts `--artifacts` for both direct implementation partitioning and
+`--contracts` source builds. It records separate group/artifact provenance,
+version-independent assembly identities and a reusable canonical owner registry.
+The old group mode remains available for comparing existing snapshots; switching
+layouts is an explicit identity migration, not CLR type equivalence. Host tests
+execute cyclic cross-artifact implementations and verify owner reservations.
+
+The semantic path reuses already referenced canonical JNI peers instead of
+regenerating them from Maven bytecode. This keeps Android platform JSON types in
+Mono.Android, including when those names occur inside typed generic signatures.
+The adjacent `.referenced-owners.json` report records the selected external
+identities; the implementation verifier rejects duplicate definitions or wrong
+reference scopes. This preserves the referenced framework API, not extra members
+found only in a newer JVM archive.
+
+The consolidated path retains native overloads whose projected CLR parameters
+coincide (for example InputStream/OutputStream), with stable names shared by the
+whole override family. Colliding opaque constructors keep their JNI entries
+through distinct factories rather than duplicate C# constructor declarations.
+
+Third-party SDK regressions also cover Kotlin serialization's `$$serializer`
+nesting, using the descriptor's JVM `InnerClasses` owner rather than guessing
+from dollar-separated names. Deep nested managed names are recursively rebased
+after enclosing interface/class projections. A concrete erased native slot does
+not collapse its typed sibling overload. ABI-changing bridges, such as a Kotlin
+Unit-returning method overriding an Object-returning generic contract, retain
+the physical Object bridge and a separately named typed entry; the compiler does
+not reinterpret a void JNI result as an object.
+Inherited abstract contracts, native bridge return shapes, and getter/setter
+projection differences remain explicit. Listener factories use qualified
+interface identities so identically named interfaces from different namespaces
+cannot generate duplicate helpers.
+
+### Reproduce
+
+Run from the repository root with Python 3.9 or later, Java, and a prepared,
+built Debug Android SDK. See the repository build instructions for preparation.
+Run comparisons sequentially when comparing generation times.
+
+```sh
+./dotnet-local.sh build external/Java.Interop/tools/generator/generator.csproj -v:minimal
+./dotnet-local.sh test external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/generator-Tests.csproj -v:minimal
+
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot activity --output /absolute/path/to/comparison-evidence
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot fragment --output /absolute/path/to/comparison-evidence
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot kotlin --output /absolute/path/to/comparison-evidence
+```
+
+The test build compiles the real Kotlin fixtures required by the Kotlin pilot.
+If using an isolated package cache, set `NUGET_PACKAGES` consistently for builds,
+tests, and comparisons.
+
+The Activity pilot uses `androidx.activity:activity:1.11.0`; Fragment uses
+`androidx.fragment:fragment:1.8.9`. Reference dependencies come from matching
+published NuGet dependency groups, **not** the upstream Maven minimum graph.
+The published binding being regenerated must not appear in its reference set.
+No pilot-specific metadata transforms are added.
+
+Each comparison creates a unique output directory containing downloaded inputs,
+the NuGet lock file, raw and adjusted XML, source, compilation and generation
+logs, recorded commands, input/reference hashes, API inventories, and
+`comparison.json`. Both modes use the same generator, raw XML, references, and
+C# compiler arguments. The SDK input build may fail at C# compilation: its
+failure is recorded, and the comparison continues only when the required
+class-parse input and generator response file exist. Both generated outputs
+are then compiled independently.
+
+Timing is the median of three fresh-process generator invocations per mode
+after one discarded warm-up invocation, with alternating order. It does not
+measure end-to-end clean-build or incremental-build cost.
+
+Coverage inventories count registered JVM types, methods, and fields, not every
+public CLR API. Activity and Fragment fail the comparison if registered APIs are
+lost or semantic output fails to compile. Kotlin instead checks its changed
+representation: three value-class structs and three distinct `Tint` overloads.
+Primitive-backed value classes have paired representations: a readonly struct
+for supported unboxed positions and a registered `XBoxed` peer for reference,
+nullable, and generic positions. Boxed peers retain their nested companions and
+methods. Both names participate in collision allocation, and signatures follow
+late type renames. Explicit boxing/unboxing helpers are not implemented yet.
+
+### Android dispatch probe
+
+Set `ANDROID_HOME` and select a running Android device or emulator explicitly:
+
+```sh
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot activity --output /absolute/path/to/comparison-evidence \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570
+```
+
+Or reuse an existing Activity comparison:
+
+```sh
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --probe-comparison /absolute/path/to/comparison.json \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570
+```
+
+The probe builds and installs `com.dotnet.bindingcompiler.spike` and requires
+all 13 checks to pass. Checks exercise typed scalar/array JNI slots, erased Java
+bridges, covariant and collection results, managed overrides and base dispatch,
+public/opaque companion fields, and `ComponentActivity` lifecycle calls.
+Per-run identities prevent accepting results from a previous installation.
+The script does not start/stop devices or uninstall the application.
+
+The native-contract pilot compiles a small Java fixture and generates its
+bindings through both pipelines:
+
+```sh
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot contracts --output /absolute/path/to/comparison-evidence \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570
+```
+
+Its nine Android checks cover an inherited Java `Iterator.remove` default
+implementation exposed as an abstract CLR interface contract, a generic
+interface implementation, a Java-to-managed callback through an interface
+diamond, synthesized abstract property access through a class
+invoker for a private native implementation, an interface-array specialization,
+public members inherited from an obfuscated-looking public base, a formatted
+generic interface adapter with a public bound, and colliding native static
+fields/methods that must both remain callable, and distinct native fields whose
+managed names collide. The fixture
+is compiled with `javac --release 8`; its source and archive hashes are recorded.
+These checks use the actual generated assembly, not hand-written binding stubs.
+
+### Boundaries
+
+The existing Java resolver and C# emitter still determine much of the result.
+Original facts are retained, but all legacy-pruned declarations are not restored.
+Some managed override signatures are erased to `Java.Lang.Object`; they are not
+a new fully generic C# facade. The current Android probe exercises valid inputs,
+not exhaustive runtime type checking or nullability.
+
+The original corpus spike does not introduce a new Kotlin metadata decoder, supported-version
+policy, default-argument adapters, complete
+boxed/value-class interoperation, or a full AndroidX/Kotlin compatibility matrix.
+Warnings and unresolved declarations remain visible in the evidence.
+The separately opt-in coroutine/Flow follow-up is described below.
+
+### Experimental Kotlin coroutine and Flow conveniences
+
+The ordinary customer-workflow reference solution is now
+[`tests/BindingProjection`](../../../../tests/BindingProjection/README.md).
+It exercises Activity/Lifecycle/DataStore, independently compiled minimal
+Reader/Writer views, canonical ownership, final native requirements validation,
+incremental builds, and a real dependency upgrade without comparison-script
+generation.
+
+`--experimental-view-contract=FILE` emits selected internal views against a
+referenced canonical owner instead of another set of registered JNI peers.
+The versioned JSON selects a JVM type and operations, the expected CLR owner,
+and the canonical managed methods to expose. Exact JNI descriptors are
+recovered from the index when unambiguous. Overloads require an explicit
+descriptor; absent or ambiguous native/managed requirements fail explicitly.
+
+`--experimental-ownership-report=FILE`, optionally with
+`--experimental-native-manifest=FILE`, records canonical JVM/CLR identities,
+Maven provenance, and directed public-contract SCC partitions. An explicit
+project owner takes precedence over suggested Maven-group boundaries.
+`--experimental-check-requirements=ASSEMBLY` validates embedded library
+requirements against the final native API and an
+`--experimental-ownership-manifest=FILE`, writing
+`--experimental-validation-report=FILE` only after successful validation.
+These options are experimental; they are not a universal JVM binary
+compatibility checker or a Roslyn source-generator authoring frontend.
+
+`--experimental-kotlin-async` adds suspend/`Task` conveniences to the
+`--experimental-semantic-model` / `XAJavaInterop1` backend. Both switches are
+required. Existing raw JNI bindings remain available, and default generation
+does not acquire an async-runtime dependency.
+
+The parser records structured classifiers, nested type arguments, variance,
+type parameters, nullability, suspend flags, and verified default/value-class
+helper descriptors from actual Kotlin metadata and bytecode. A trailing
+`Continuation` alone does not prove a suspend function. Immutable semantic
+lowering retains constructor facts as well as methods without changing the JVM
+descriptor. Metadata 1.1-1.9 and 2.0-2.4 are recognized; unsupported versions
+produce a diagnostic and preserve raw bindings without guessed Kotlin facts.
+
+The compiler emits `Task` for non-null `Unit`, and `Task<T>` for strings, all
+eight JVM primitives, resolved peers, and nested List/Set/Map views, preserving
+nullable results/elements. Unsupported generic forms retain opaque peers.
+Native names ending in `Async` are preserved; conflicting conveniences receive
+a `KotlinAsync` suffix, and overloads stay together.
+
+The canonical optional runtime/package is
+[`src/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin`](../../../../src/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin/README.md).
+Its public boundary uses opaque JNI peers and has no managed Kotlin/coroutines
+package dependencies. It works with published or regenerated Kotlin owners.
+The NuGet contains the managed assembly and authored native source, **no JAR/AAR
+payload**. Include that source project once in the existing app Gradle graph;
+do not create a second private native dependency graph or emit runtime classes
+in every binding assembly. This remains an opt-in experimental feature, not a
+workload-wide dependency or a published shipping package.
+
+For example, the generated fixture supports:
+
+```csharp
+using Java.Interop.Kotlin;
+
+string text = await fixture.DelayedAsync (cancellationToken);
+int number = await fixture.NumberAsync (cancellationToken);
+await fixture.UnitAsync (cancellationToken);
+
+await foreach (string? item in fixture.FiniteAsAsyncEnumerable (cancellationToken)) {
+    // A null item is a value, not end-of-stream.
+}
+```
+
+Flow methods and getter properties receive typed `IAsyncEnumerable<T>` facades.
+Explicit-converter `AsAsyncEnumerable<T>` and raw-peer enumeration also remain.
+Nullable Flow results remain nullable, not empty streams.
+
+Verified `$default` methods produce `WithDefaults` / `WithDefaultsAsync`
+facades. `KotlinOptional<T>` distinguishes omission from null/zero; native Kotlin
+evaluates default expressions. Public concrete constructors receive
+`CreateWithDefaults` factories. Value-class `Box`/`Unbox` preserve representation;
+`Create`/`CreateValue` invoke actual constructor validation. Managed suspend
+implementations use `KotlinCoroutines.FromTask`, including original managed
+exceptions and native cancellation that waits for managed cleanup. Arbitrary
+reverse implementation classes are not autogenerated.
+
+Runtime contracts:
+
+- Kotlin executes the suspend ABI, including immediate completion, suspended
+  completion, exceptions, and Kotlin's internal result representation. The
+  generated method does not wrap blocking work in `Task.Run`.
+- A cancellation token cancels the owned native coroutine. A cancelled Task is
+  delivered after native completion and `finally` blocks, not merely when the
+  .NET token changes. Cancellation remains cooperative.
+- An optional `coroutineContext` accepts a native dispatcher and parent Job.
+  The adapter owns a supervised child scope: parent cancellation propagates
+  down, but a failed Task does not cancel the caller's Job.
+- Without an explicit native context, `Dispatchers.Unconfined` starts on the
+  calling thread and does **not** promise main-thread resumption. A captured
+  .NET synchronization context is not automatically a Kotlin dispatcher.
+  UI-sensitive native code must receive the appropriate native context;
+  Android Main dispatch also requires the usual coroutines-android module.
+- Flow collection is lazy and each enumeration starts a new native collection.
+  One native subscription plus demand acknowledgement prevents hidden prefetch while
+  the managed consumer processes its current value. Native hot-flow
+  conflation/replay rules are not replaced with a managed event queue.
+- Async disposal, early `break`, converter failure, and token cancellation
+  cancel and join collection, including native cleanup. Cleanup is not
+  skipped because the consumer's token or native parent is cancelled.
+- Returned peers are not indiscriminately disposed; the adapter preserves
+  existing canonical peers and caller ownership.
+- A reusable `ManualResetValueTaskSourceCore<bool>` replaces the old per-item
+  operation/Task/FlowItem protocol. The device benchmark runs 10,000 items in
+  three alternating trials per protocol, checks count/checksum, and requires at
+  least 20% lower median managed allocation. Measurements exclude Java/native
+  heaps and are not shipping-device performance guarantees.
+
+Build the generator tests to compile the real Kotlin fixture, then run:
+
+```sh
+./dotnet-local.sh test external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/generator-Tests.csproj -v minimal
+./dotnet-local.sh build external/Java.Interop/tools/class-parse/class-parse.csproj -v minimal
+./dotnet-local.sh build src/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin.csproj -v minimal
+
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot async --iterations 1 \
+    --output /absolute/path/to/async-evidence \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570
+
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --probe-comparison /absolute/path/to/async-evidence/async-RUN/comparison.json \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570 --probe-configuration Release
+
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --probe-comparison /absolute/path/to/async-evidence/async-RUN/comparison.json \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570 --probe-configuration Release --probe-nativeaot
+```
+
+Set `ANDROID_HOME` and use the session's isolated `NUGET_PACKAGES` cache.
+Release enables full trimming and R8. Identity-tagged logcat results work for
+non-debuggable optimized packages. The production driver requires 56 distinct passing
+checks covering result shapes, native failures, cancellation races, native
+contexts including Android Main/immediate, cleanup, null items, cold/hot flows,
+strict backpressure, managed suspend implementations, defaults, boxing,
+generic collection views, and allocation measurements. The original fixture
+runtime remains only as a historical per-item benchmark baseline.
+
+To compile the same real fixture with another Kotlin compiler without running
+an old Kotlin Gradle plugin on the current Gradle:
+
+```sh
+bash build-tools/gradle/gradlew --no-daemon \
+    -p external/Java.Interop/tests/Xamarin.Android.Tools.Bytecode-Tests/kotlin-compatibility \
+    compileCompatibility -PkotlinCompatibilityVersion=2.0.21 \
+    -PkotlinCompatibilityOutput=/absolute/path/to/compatibility/classes
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-bindings.py \
+    --pilot async --iterations 1 \
+    --kotlin-classes /absolute/path/to/compatibility/classes \
+    --output /absolute/path/to/compatibility/results --device-serial emulator-5570
+```
+
+Compiler/device fixtures cover 2.0.21, 2.1.20, and 2.4.21, not every recognized
+metadata version or Kotlin language feature.
+
+The independent NuGet/source-graph smoke check uses no managed Kotlin bindings:
+
+```sh
+./dotnet-local.sh pack src/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin.csproj \
+    -o /absolute/path/to/local-packages
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/verify-kotlin-package.py \
