+
+It rejects native binary payloads/managed dependencies, builds the packaged
+source in one Gradle graph, verifies selected stdlib 2.4.0 and an incremental
+build without modifying the package directory, and runs three Android checks.
+
+### Full configured Maven corpus
+
+`compare-binding-corpus.py` expands the comparison to every artifact listed in an upstream
+`dotnet/android-libraries/config.json`. Supply a saved configuration and its
+actual source commit, rather than a moving branch name:
+
+```sh
+python3 -m unittest discover \
+    -s external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests \
+    -p 'test_compare_binding_corpus.py' -v
+
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-binding-corpus.py \
+    --config /absolute/path/to/config.json \
+    --config-commit 2393441675a6d82bb817ab7aa2df698c7ec929ce \
+    --output /absolute/path/to/corpus-evidence \
+    --jobs 4 \
+    --descriptor-tool external/Java.Interop/tools/jvm-api-descriptor/build/install/jvm-api-descriptor/lib \
+    --generic-runtime src/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin/bin/Debug/net11.0-android/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin.dll
+```
+
+Build the descriptor tool and canonical runtime first when supplying these
+optional arguments. Omitting them retains the original corpus comparison.
+Each run snapshots both tool dependencies and the runtime DLL. Expected
+supported-API exclusions are reported separately from unexplained losses;
+compilation alone is not a functional-equivalence verdict.
+
+That pinned configuration contains 717 entries: 350 AndroidX, 94 Google Play
+Services, 59 Firebase and 214 other records across 161 Maven groups. Earlier
+444-record runs covered only AndroidX/GMS and are not full-config results.
+Entries marked `dependencyOnly` or `no-bindings`
+remain in the accounting. Configured Maven Central versus Google repositories
+are honored, with the shared public Maven mirror used on CI. Publisher-declared
+Maven relocations are recorded distinctly from empty archives.
+To narrow a rerun, repeat `--artifact group:artifact`,
+for example `--artifact androidx.activity:activity`.
+
+The harness caches artifact preparation, snapshots the generator tool directory,
+and identifies comparison runs by tool and driver hashes. Repeating a command
+resumes saved results; changing generator binaries creates a separate run while
+reusing prepared inputs. Use a new output directory when changing class-parse
+behavior, because cached raw API XML is not reparsed automatically.
+
+Each run's `summary.json` records per-artifact results. An input or compilation
+failure is not a successful comparison. No-JVM artifacts, empty projections,
+and registered API losses have distinct statuses; compilable output alone is
+not evidence of completeness. Native registration inventories do not capture
+all managed API changes or prove correct dispatch and marshaling.
+
+These are independent-library comparisons against matching **published NuGet
+dependency DLLs**, which may contain upstream manual corrections. They do not
+establish that a regenerated dependency graph compiles or works on Android;
+use the separate closure experiment below for that evidence.
+
+When preparation reports a NuGet downgrade, the harness can promote a direct
+dependency to a concrete stable version only if it satisfies every observed
+declared range. Original constraints, promotion evidence, and both build logs
+are preserved. Exact/exclusive bounds are not relaxed, prerelease reconciliation
+is unsupported, and incompatible graphs remain explicit input failures.
+
+### Regenerated native dependency closure
+
+`compare-binding-closure.py` consumes an **already resolved** native dependency
+graph. It does not replace Gradle dependency resolution. Its input JSON is an
+array of records with `group`, `artifact`, `version`, and absolute `file` paths
+to the selected JAR/AAR archives. Preserve the resolver's lockfile alongside
+this evidence.
+
+By default the harness regenerates AndroidX/GMS artifacts into one
+`BindingClosure.dll`, with other support bindings supplied by a NuGet assets
+file. `--regenerate-support` instead regenerates the entire resolved graph,
+including Kotlin, coroutines, Guava, and annotation libraries, without published
+support binding DLLs. This tests common-assembly ownership, not independent
+per-artifact assemblies or every possible version combination.
+
+```sh
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-binding-closure.py \
+    --native-inputs /absolute/path/to/resolved-native-inputs.json \
+    --support-assets /absolute/path/to/support-project/obj/project.assets.json \
+    --tools-from /absolute/path/to/corpus-run/summary.json \
+    --output /absolute/path/to/closure-evidence
+```
+
+For complete support regeneration, replace `--support-assets ...` with
+`--regenerate-support`. Add `--kotlin-runtime /absolute/path/to/Microsoft.Android.Kotlin.dll`
+to enable Kotlin conveniences throughout the semantic closure, including the
+regenerated Kotlin standard library. The canonical runtime remains a separate
+owner. A resolved record may set `bind: false` for its already-owned native
+bridge; it remains a native input but is not rebound into the closure assembly.
+
+The selected corpus summary supplies an immutable compiler snapshot. Both
+pipelines receive the same raw XML and support references. Archive and support
+assembly hashes, source, commands, diagnostics, and `comparison.json` are saved.
+Missing or conflicting support assemblies fail explicitly; assets from other
+target frameworks are not imported.
+
+The AndroidX fixture requires a native graph containing EmojiPicker 1.7.0.
+It derives from regenerated AppCompat and checks common
+assembly ownership, Activity/Fragment/Lifecycle/SavedState access, actual Java
+lifecycle callbacks into a managed observer, a default interface method with
+an inaccessible native ancestor, and Kotlin EmojiPicker construction/property
+round trips, plus regenerated unsigned Kotlin boundary conversions. A second
+six-check fixture covers Okio 3.9.1, OkHttp 4.12.0, RxJava 2.2.21/3.1.10,
+Gson 2.11.0, and protobuf-javalite 3.25.5 with their resolved native dependencies:
+
+```sh
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-binding-closure.py \
+    --probe-comparison /absolute/path/to/closure/comparison.json \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570
+
+# Repeat with full trimming and R8:
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-binding-closure.py \
+    --probe-comparison /absolute/path/to/closure/comparison.json \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570 --probe-configuration Release
+
+# Repeat with NativeAOT, full trimming, and R8:
+python3 external/Java.Interop/tests/generator-Tests/compare-binding-closure.py \
+    --probe-comparison /absolute/path/to/closure/comparison.json \
+    --device-serial emulator-5570 --probe-configuration Release --probe-nativeaot
+```
+
+Set `ANDROID_HOME` and use a running device. Native archives must still match
+their recorded hashes. Support DLLs are staged without adjacent NuGet AARs,
+and their embedded native-resource extraction is disabled, so the app packages
+the resolved native graph rather than a second graph from published bindings.
+The fixture runs after native activity creation and requires six distinct,
+identity-tagged successes plus its final completion marker. Results are read
+from logcat in all configurations, including non-debuggable Release packages.
+Each run saves its project, frozen fixture, logs, and device result JSON.
+The script does not start/stop devices or uninstall
+`com.dotnet.bindingcompiler.closure`.
+
+The verified EmojiPicker graph selects Emoji2 1.7.0 through Gradle module
+metadata. Its incompatible published NuGet dependency graph remains a failed
+independent-library input; closure success does not reclassify that restore.
+
+The semantic managed-contract pass also reconciles imported names, actual
+property metadata, enum slots, generic erasure, and Java visibility widening.
+Public forwarding methods preserve access to Java methods whose C# override
+must remain protected. Legacy callback connectors keep their registration
+format but use fixed-length native-identity hashes for source-owned semantic
+projections, including the native return descriptor. Compact callback allocation
+also distinguishes these native identities. Imported connectors stay unchanged;
+unboxed Kotlin callback arguments are reconstructed as their projected structs.
+
+Missing generic interface slots on abstract classes become actual abstract
+declarations, not throwing placeholders. When a projected property or an erased
+base override cannot directly implement an interface method, an explicit JNI
+invoker retains the interface contract. Imported generic collections use their
+typed list/set/map marshalers rather than open CLR generic definitions.
+This includes nested collections and the concrete ArrayList, HashSet, and HashMap
+types created by the existing Android runtime marshalers. Actual referenced
+managed return types, array element types, and primitive aliases remain distinct
+from their native descriptors. Setter-only Java overrides retain inherited C#
+property shapes; inaccessible interfaces do not define phantom managed contracts.
+Explicit JNI properties retain inherited mutable interface accessors and default
+Java implementations that published C# interfaces expose as abstract contracts.
+Default interface overrides are relinked after managed enum/type reconciliation.
+Read-only base properties keep their CLR shape when a Java subclass adds a setter:
+the new setter remains a callable method. Public JNI peer bounds retain typed
+generic constructor/return positions and follow subsequent managed name allocation.
+
+Duplicate copies of the same native slot are coalesced. Distinct compiler bridges
+that collide with a typed method's managed signature retain their JNI descriptors
+under nonvirtual `Bridge` aliases. Exact bridge target decoding is not implemented;
+name/arity bridge association remains a limitation. Overloaded listener methods
+retain their method names while receiving distinct event/delegate/argument names.
+Invalid namespace segments are encoded reversibly, and interface peer-member
+field names use native-identity hashes.
+Argument types are allocated across unrelated listeners, and multiple listener
+connectors receive distinct event conveniences and independent implementor fields,
+including typed removal for overloaded setters. Static factories, fluent instance
+methods, and properties retain distinct managed names without dropping native
+slots. Actual Java string overloads take priority over duplicate generated
+string conveniences, and erased signatures do not produce identical conveniences.
+Semantic class invokers implement the actual emitted abstract declarations rather
+than relying solely on the original JVM or imported CLR abstract flags.
+This inventory includes properties as well as methods. Imported bases contribute
+their actual CLR declarations; source ancestors can also contribute synthesized
+interface declarations. Explicit JNI adapters must not become phantom overrides.
+Generic interface adapters dispatch through their exact interface JNI slots
+instead of assuming that a separately projected typed C# target exists.
+String conveniences only retype direct CharSequence and CharSequence-array
+parameters, not nested generic collection elements with unchanged marshalers.
+Concrete native peers retain missing explicit interface contracts even when
+their managed bases do not expose the corresponding inherited/default method.
+Semantic type eligibility respects explicit `obfuscated="true"` exclusions,
+not guesses based on short or `zz`-prefixed names: public bases with such names
+can be necessary for other public APIs.
+Synthetic implementations are retained when they supply an exact abstract base
+or interface slot, even if an obfuscator has cleared their JVM bridge flag.
+When a real synthetic bridge implements an exact abstract native base slot and
+no existing typed projection covers it, its own entry point is retained for
+source and imported bases. This does not infer a bridge's typed target or
+replace the still-needed conservative bridge-bytecode analysis.
+Nested types cannot reuse their enclosing managed type name, and fields cannot
+hide inherited method contracts needed by downstream overrides. Erased final
+overloads can receive aliases without renaming a required base override.
+A derived type that overrides an inherited property of the same managed name
+receives a type alias before property planning. This preserves the property
+override rather than emitting an incompatible method override; merely inheriting
+such a property does not rename the type.
+Inherited interface callbacks select the original declaring CLR interface to
+avoid ambiguous calls through multiply inherited interface members.
+Public visibility facades for omitted variant properties invoke their native
+slots directly instead of assigning to an unrelated inherited typed property.
+Explicit generic JNI adapters use the final formatted CLR method name.
+Field naming preserves distinct native fields even when their projected names
+collide with each other or with newly recovered static methods.
+Public interface ancestors remain CLR contracts when an intermediate native
+interface is inaccessible. Default methods do not explicitly implement omitted
+private interfaces. Listener conveniences do not generate Java implementors
+for interfaces that cannot be named from an external Java package, including
+public interfaces nested inside inaccessible owners.
+Final/static overloads whose CLR signatures erase identically retain separate,
+descriptively named methods and their original JNI descriptors. High-arity
+callbacks pass native arguments as a value tuple through the existing guarded
+JNI transition helpers instead of requiring a helper overload for every arity.
+Signatures referencing unprojectable nonpublic peers use opaque Java object
+peers without changing native descriptors, including constructor parameters.
+This intentionally weakens CLR typing and still needs API-usability and device
+validation; it is not evidence that the projection is functionally better.
+Protected nested classes also flatten inaccessible Java bases using the existing
+public-class rule rather than inheriting missing managed types.
+Abstract peers retain public operations inherited from inaccessible Java
+interfaces as ordinary class declarations and JNI invokers, not references to
+missing CLR interfaces. Existing public class operations take priority over
+private-interface fluent-return contracts. Kotlin's Java-source override
+restriction does not suppress required CLR overrides of abstract mangled slots;
+managed-subclass Java callable wrappers for JVM-only names remain a runtime
+validation concern.
+
+All these rules are experimental and require corpus and runtime validation.
